1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,234
FeCheck: property-based and state-machine testing, written in Fe, for Fe.

2
00:00:06,133 --> 00:00:09,813
Everything you see runs on the Fe twenty-six point six compiler.

3
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:23,005
We search a thousand random operation sequences against a permission system. Case forty-five fails, and FeCheck shrinks it from seven operations to four. The violation: a revoked agent is still allowed to execute.

4
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:27,950
The run also prints a regression test, ready to paste into fe test.

5
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:36,286
The shrunk failure is saved as a case file that pins the compiler and FeCheck versions.

6
00:00:38,967 --> 00:00:44,447
Replay that file against the same buggy system, and the failure is reproduced at operation four.

7
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:56,294
Replay it against the fixed system, and it is not reproduced. Exit code three is exactly what we expect.

8
00:01:00,167 --> 00:01:19,156
The same machine model runs on two backends. The native Fe build finds the failure. The case file is byte-identical to the one embedded in the contract test, and fe test replays it on the EVM: it reproduces on the buggy contract and passes on the fixed one. Then one million native cases run in about half a second.

9
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:26,700
On a real ERC-20 fixture from upstream Fe, a one-line mutant is caught by the same kind of model.

10
00:01:31,933 --> 00:01:41,837
FeCheck. Find the failure, shrink the story, replay the proof. A hundred eighty-one EVM tests, plus native suites, on Fe twenty-six point six.

